I’d study how an idea earns a place in someone else’s project. That takes the art of flanking past marketing and into the design of the Pantheon itself. Imagine someone arrives because they want to turn a math problem into a comedy skit. What helps them find Math to Myth, try it in ten minutes, and carry the method into their own work?

Topic	Question it teaches us to ask	What it could improve in the repo
Information foraging	What clues tell a newcomer they’re about to find something useful?	Index by what someone wants to do, alongside the canon names. “Turn an equation into a character scene” leads to Math to Myth.
Boundary objects	What can people from different fields work on together while each sees something different in it?	Give a piece like The King’s Imaginary Seat three ways in. A writer sees a scene, a math reader sees the underlying structure, and a builder sees a method they can implement.
Adoption and compatibility	How much of the system does somebody need before they can put one piece to work?	Make selected modules portable as single Markdown files with a worked example, required context, and a way to check whether the method worked.
Recommender systems and serendipity	Which neighboring artifact would change what someone does next?	Add a few links that explain the payoff of crossing domains. Math to Myth gives a character a problem. Semantic Surface Area can help explore how the same scene reads differently to different audiences.
Organizational memory	What will a future reader need to know about how this idea came to exist?	Preserve the initiating question, key transformations, and which choices came from you or were introduced by the model where that history is available. That gives the silent half of the Pantheon a trace a reader can follow.
Recombination and constraints	What can change when we move an idea to a new medium, and what has to survive?	Give modules a section for their invariants. A Math to Myth comedy sketch can change personalities, setting, and tone while preserving the mathematical dependency that makes the ending work.
Causal inference	Did an indirect route actually help someone use the work?	Compare task-based entry points with a canon index. Give readers the same task and see which route helps them produce a usable result. Stars, visits, and completed uses answer different questions.

The three I’d study first are information foraging, boundary objects, and organizational memory. Together they address a particular challenge for the Pantheon. A stranger should be able to find one useful piece, understand it through their own craft, and still see enough of its origins to adapt it intelligently.

I’d test that on one artifact before touching the broader repo. Give Math to Myth a task-based doorway called “Turn an equation into a scene.” Start with one equation, show the resulting skit, annotate which mathematical relationships became character relationships, then hand the reader a second equation to try. Close with a link to Semantic Surface Area and a concrete reason to follow it: “You’ve made the scene work. Now see how changing the audience changes what the scene means.”

That gives you a test of both sides of the idea. Can someone use a piece of the Pantheon on its own, and does using it give them a genuine reason to discover the next piece?